## PHASE 2: Build the Page

---

### Step 1: Map the Buyer

Identify which awareness stage the target reader is at. Services pages for competitive B2B keywords almost always target Solution-aware buyers — people who have five tabs open comparing agencies and are looking for a reason to choose one over the others.

| Stage | Who they are | Page implication |
|---|---|---|
| **Solution-aware** | Evaluating options | Lead with differentiation. Comparison, process, proof. |
| **Product-aware** | Checking you specifically | Lead with proof and objection handling. Trust signals early. |
| **Problem-aware** | Know the pain, not the solution type | Problem section earns more real estate. Solution introduction is deliberate. |

Default to Solution-aware unless the keyword or brief strongly signals otherwise.

---

### Step 2: Determine Section Architecture

Build section order from the brief and competitor gaps. Do not use a fixed template.

**Mandatory sections — always present:**

| Section | Rule |
|---|---|
| **Hero** | Headline names who this is for and what outcome they get. Not a tagline. Includes primary CTA button. |
| **Who this is for** | Specific company types, stages, or situations. Not "businesses of all sizes." |
| **Not a fit for** | Always required. See rule below. |
| **Closing CTA** | Last section. Follows from the page's argument. Not a disconnected banner. |

**Not a fit for — the rule:**

This section builds more trust than the hero. Anyone can write a compelling pitch. An agency that says "we are not right for you if X" signals that they mean the rest of it too.

Write at least three specific, real disqualifiers. Reject vague hedges. Require specificity:
- Revenue or contract value thresholds where the economics do not work
- Internal approval or bandwidth requirements that kill execution momentum
- Business stages that are too early or too large for this service to fit
- Industries or models where this service type genuinely underperforms

Source these from the user's Round 2 disqualifier answers. If the user did not answer, ask before writing this section.

**Conditional sections — include when justified:**

| Section | Include when |
|---|---|
| Problem / cost of inaction | Audience is Problem-aware, or no competitor addresses the cost of the status quo |
| Process / timeline | Competitors skip timeline clarity (High gap), or service complexity makes process a buying signal |
| Deliverables table | Buyers need to know exactly what they receive each month |
| Transparent pricing | Always include if user provides pricing data |
| Agency vs in-house vs freelancer | Include when buyer realistically considers all three |
| Case studies / proof | Include when user provides real named case studies. If not, describe client type and results honestly without fabricating specifics. |
| AI / current trend angle | Include when the industry has a meaningful recent shift buyers are actively navigating |
| FAQ | Always include. Minimum 6 questions. Write the real objections a skeptical buyer would raise. |

**Section order logic:**

Sequence sections around the buyer's decision process. A Solution-aware buyer does not need an education section. They need: who you work with, how you work, what it costs, whether you are right for them, what happens after they contact you.

When competitor gaps are available: cover the highest-opportunity gaps early in the page, not buried at the bottom.

---

### Step 3: Write the Page

#### Voice and Tone

Expert, approachable, conversational. A practitioner who knows this cold and has no need to over-sell.

You sit on the same side of the table as the reader. You do not frame their current situation as a failure. You do not attack their existing vendor or process. You describe the problem they probably recognise from the inside — then you show a better path.

Sentences: mix short and long. No paragraph walls. Every section has a clear job. Nothing is there because it sounds nice.

Metaphors and analogies: grounded in the reader's world — their industry, their day-to-day workflow, their business stage.

#### What to Do

- **Hero headline:** Who it's for + what outcome they get. Or: what you do differently + for whom. Not a slogan.
- **Problem framing:** Name the specific friction, not the category. Not "SEO can be complex." Instead: "Your agency sends a monthly report. Rankings are up. Traffic is up. Sales says nothing changed."
- **Process sections:** Show what actually happens, in sequence. Buyers are trying to imagine working with you. Make it concrete enough to imagine.
- **Pricing:** Include if user provided data. Acknowledge scope variance. Tell the reader they will get a fixed number after the scoping call.
- **FAQ:** One clear answer per question. Not a paragraph-length hedge. Not a redirect to "contact us."
- **Proof:** Named case studies with real outcomes when available. If unavailable, describe client type and result category honestly — no fabricated specifics.

#### What Not to Do

See The Blacklist below.

---

### Claim Integrity Rules

Non-negotiable. Violating any of these is worse than not making the claim at all.

**On statistics:**
- No stat without a traceable, named source
- If you cannot source a stat, describe the observable pattern qualitatively instead
- Do not compound unverified stats into calculations
- Figures that vary by industry, role, or company size must acknowledge that variance

**On strong performance claims:**
- Any claim of faster / better / cheaper than the alternative must be immediately followed by the mechanism that produces it
- If you cannot explain the mechanism, soften the claim or remove it

**On service-specific terminology:**
- Every technical or proprietary term defined plainly on first use
- Vague feature language with no explanation erodes trust faster than no claim at all
- Integration claims name specific platforms — "your existing stack" is not a claim

**On problem framing:**
- Every problem statement must pass the Competitor Test: could this sentence appear on a rival's page without changing a word? If yes, rewrite it.
- Category-level truisms are not insights. Replace with the specific mechanism or specific consequence.
- No audience-excluding conditional framing in headlines or section intros

